{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Google BigQuery?

**Google BigQuery is a fully managed analytical data warehouse.** It stores large
datasets and runs SQL across them without asking a team to provision database servers.
Applications, pipelines, and analysts submit jobs; BigQuery plans the work, assigns
parallel compute called **slots**, reads columnar data, and returns a result.

BigQuery matters when one question must summarize millions or billions of records:
revenue by market, product usage by cohort, security events by signature, or model
features over months of history. It is built for analytical scans and aggregations,
not for the small row-by-row transactions that power a checkout or account balance.

The central design rule is simple: **query latency and cost follow the work the query
creates.** Data layout can reduce bytes read, SQL shape can reduce shuffle, and workload
management can keep important jobs from competing with every other query.

{% callout variant="info" %}
If projects, IAM, service accounts, and regions are unfamiliar, review the
[Google Cloud foundations lesson](/technology/gcp) first. This lesson explains the
BigQuery-specific decisions from first principles.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## How a BigQuery query runs

A query is not executed by one large database process. BigQuery turns SQL into stages,
divides each stage into parallel work units, and assigns slots to those units. Stages
exchange intermediate records through distributed shuffle before the final result is
assembled.

{% system-flow title="From SQL to an analytical result" caption="The planner can prune data before execution, but joins, grouping, sorting, and concurrency still determine how much slot and shuffle work remains." %}
{% system-node title="SQL job" eyebrow="Request" icon="code" tone="blue" %}
Authorize the caller, resolve tables and views, and validate the statement.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Execution plan" eyebrow="Plan" icon="braces" tone="violet" %}
Choose stages, prune eligible partitions and blocks, and divide work into parallel units.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Slots + shuffle" eyebrow="Execute" icon="server" tone="cyan" %}
Read columns, join and aggregate records, and exchange intermediate data between stages.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Result + telemetry" eyebrow="Observe" icon="activity" tone="green" %}
Return rows and record bytes processed, slot milliseconds, wait time, and stage behavior.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Storage is columnar" icon="storage" tone="blue" %}
Analytical queries can read referenced columns instead of every field in each row.
Partitioning and clustering can reduce the relevant storage blocks further.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Slots are an accounting unit" icon="speed" tone="violet" %}
A slot represents compute capacity for query work. One query can use many slots over
time, so `total_slot_ms` is more useful than counting queries alone.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Shuffle connects stages" icon="network" tone="amber" %}
Joins and aggregations move intermediate data. Skewed keys or explosive joins can
create high shuffle pressure even after the initial table scan is small.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Convert query work into slot pressure

Capacity planning starts with observed work, not a universal bytes-per-slot rule. For
a completed query, divide `total_slot_ms` by 1,000 to get slot-seconds. A steady workload
with `Q` queries per minute and `S` slot-seconds per query demands approximately
`Q x S / 60` average slots.

This estimate exposes three different decisions:

- **Query decision:** reduce slot-seconds by pruning inputs, aggregating earlier, or
  removing unnecessary shuffle.
- **Scheduling decision:** move batch work away from interactive peaks or isolate it in
  another reservation.
- **Capacity decision:** add baseline or autoscaling capacity when optimized work still
  exceeds the service target.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Average utilization hides bursts and skew. Leave measured headroom and inspect p95 wait
time, timeline demand, and queue behavior before treating 100% average utilization as a
safe operating target.
{% /callout %}

{% interactive-block id="technology/bigquery-slot-cost-query-pressure-lab" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Partitioning and clustering reduce different work

**Partitioning** divides a table into coarse segments, commonly by day. A query with a
usable predicate on the partition column can skip unrelated segments. **Clustering**
organizes storage blocks by selected columns inside the table; selective filters may
then skip blocks that cannot contain matching values.

Use the query pattern to choose the layout:

1. Start with a stable date or timestamp that appears in most bounded queries.
2. Add clustering for selective filter or join columns used repeatedly inside those
   partitions.
3. Require a partition filter when an accidental full-retention scan is unacceptable.
4. Confirm the result with dry-run bytes and production job history. Clustering is
   block pruning, not a guaranteed index lookup.

The next lab uses a 90 TiB table retained for 365 days. Its clustered reduction is an
explicit planning assumption so the reader can compare decisions; real block selectivity
depends on data distribution and table evolution.

{% interactive-block id="technology/bigquery-partition-failure-optimization-lab" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Place BigQuery in a real analytics system

Consider a retail platform that needs operational dashboards, finance reports, and
churn features from the same governed history. The serving application remains on a
transactional database; analytical events and replicated business facts flow into
BigQuery asynchronously.

{% system-flow title="Retail analytics path" caption="Each stage has a different responsibility: durable ingestion, trustworthy transformation, efficient storage layout, and controlled consumption." %}
{% system-node title="Events + source changes" eyebrow="Produce" icon="activity" tone="blue" %}
Applications emit events while change-data capture exports committed business facts.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Raw landing tables" eyebrow="Ingest" icon="storage" tone="cyan" %}
Preserve source fields, ingestion metadata, and replay boundaries for correction.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Curated models" eyebrow="Transform" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
Deduplicate, apply business definitions, and partition tables around common access paths.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="BI, ML, and exports" eyebrow="Consume" icon="activity" tone="green" %}
Give each workload governed views, appropriate reservations, and freshness expectations.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Good workload fits

- Enterprise reporting over large historical datasets.
- Product and customer analytics that aggregate many records.
- Log, audit, and security analysis with bounded retention queries.
- Batch feature engineering and SQL-accessible machine learning.
- Streaming analytics when consumers can tolerate warehouse-style query latency.

### Use another serving system when

- A user request needs predictable single-row reads in a few milliseconds.
- Many concurrent transactions must update related records atomically.
- The application depends on frequent point writes and immediate key lookups.
- A regional request path cannot tolerate the warehouse job and result lifecycle.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Design for failures at the correct boundary

BigQuery manages machines, storage replication within a location, and much of query
execution. The application team still owns query admission, workload isolation, data
recovery requirements, and regional continuity.

- **Queries wait for slots.**
  - Evidence: stage wait time rises, reservation demand approaches capacity, and queued
    jobs grow during overlapping workloads.
  - Response: isolate critical workloads, move batch schedules, reduce query work, or
    add measured capacity.
- **A scan regresses.**
  - Evidence: processed bytes jump after a date predicate changes or upstream retention
    grows.
  - Response: use dry runs, require partition filters, and alert on bytes processed by
    stable query fingerprints.
- **Shuffle spills or becomes skewed.**
  - Evidence: a join or aggregation emits far more intermediate data than expected,
    with a small set of stages dominating slot time.
  - Response: inspect key frequency, deduplicate or aggregate earlier, and avoid
    many-to-many joins that multiply rows unintentionally.
- **A query fails after submission.**
  - Evidence: the job has an error while the client does not know whether a prior
    submission was accepted.
  - Response: use stable job IDs for safe retry behavior, classify retryable errors, and
    make downstream writes idempotent.
- **A region becomes unavailable.**
  - Evidence: data and compute in the selected location cannot serve the workload.
  - Response: meet a regional RTO only with a geographically separate data copy,
    usable compute capacity, routing, and a tested failover procedure.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
A multi-region location is a placement choice, not proof that the workload has an
independent cross-region replica and failover path. Define the recovery point and
recovery time first, then verify the architecture satisfies both.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Operate from workload telemetry

Track the system at table, query, reservation, and business boundaries. A useful review
connects resource evidence to a user-visible promise instead of alerting on every large
number.

### A practical BigQuery optimization loop

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Measure" label="Observe" %}
Group recurring jobs by workload and record bytes, slot time, wait time, latency, and errors.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Locate" label="Diagnose" %}
Open the execution graph and find the scan, join, aggregate, sort, or shuffle stage that dominates work.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Change" label="Experiment" %}
Modify one boundary: SQL shape, table layout, schedule, reservation assignment, or capacity.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Verify" label="Compare" %}
Compare the same workload window and preserve the change only when cost and service targets improve.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Production review checklist

- Query controls
  - Use maximum-bytes limits or dry runs where a broad scan would be expensive.
  - Label jobs so owners and workloads can be identified in telemetry.
  - Set explicit timeouts and retry only errors documented as transient.
- Data controls
  - Monitor partition freshness, late-arriving data, schema drift, and retention.
  - Test deletion recovery and any cross-region replication procedure.
  - Keep sensitive columns behind least-privilege views and policy controls.
- Capacity controls
  - Review reservation assignments before assuming a project uses intended slots.
  - Separate latency-sensitive BI from opportunistic transformations when contention is visible.
  - Compare baseline, autoscaling, and on-demand economics with actual workload curves.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Choose the pricing and layout trade-offs deliberately

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="On-demand analysis" eyebrow="Variable work" icon="activity" tone="blue" %}
Useful when processed bytes are intermittent or hard to predict. Cost control depends on
pruning, query limits, and ownership because every broad scan is visible.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Capacity reservations" eyebrow="Predictable compute" icon="server" tone="violet" %}
Useful for steady or isolated workloads. Baseline slots improve planning, but idle
capacity and poorly assigned projects can waste the commitment.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="More partitions" eyebrow="Pruning" icon="storage" tone="green" %}
Well-chosen partitions bound common queries and retention. A poor partition key or tiny
partitions add management overhead without matching access patterns.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="More clustering columns" eyebrow="Block locality" icon="layers" tone="amber" %}
Clustering can reduce block reads for repeated selective predicates. It does not fix a
missing partition filter, a row-multiplying join, or regional availability.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

The best configuration is observable and reversible. Start from query history, make one
change, and compare bytes, slot work, wait time, and user latency over the same workload
window.
{% /section-card %}

